# Byte-exact fixtures: two local gates compare these files byte-for-byte against
# generated output (`key_normalization_fixture` in packages/core, `check-policy-census.sh`).
# Their committed blobs are LF; without pinning, Windows autocrlf checks the working tree
# out as CRLF and both gates false-fail locally while Linux CI stays green. Pin instead of
# repo-wide `* text=auto` to keep this a targeted fix (no mass renormalization).
# The convention-vocab baseline is regenerated the same way (`--update-baseline` rewrites it
# whole and the guard greps it line-exact), so it needs the same pin.
# The detection-benchmark coverage floor is the same shape again: benchmark.mjs rewrites it whole with
# `\n` on every --update-baseline, so without the pin a Windows working tree turns every line into a
# spurious CRLF diff the first time the floor is raised.
scripts/policy-census.txt text eol=lf
scripts/convention-vocab-baseline.txt text eol=lf
scripts/detection-expected-baseline.txt text eol=lf
docs/adapters/*.fixture.json text eol=lf

# The plugin's SessionStart hook, pinned for the same reason in a nastier place: Claude Code clones
# this repo as a marketplace onto the USER's machine, and on Windows (autocrlf on by default in Git
# for Windows) that checkout would rewrite the script as CRLF. A `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` shebang is
# the classic "bad interpreter" failure, on exactly the platform where this hook is already the most
# fragile — and the hook was verified working as LF, so pin what was tested.
.claude-plugin/hooks/*.sh text eol=lf
